          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Akira Kurosawa</strong><br>\nAkira Kurosawa was born on March 23, 1910, in Tokyo, Japan. Growing up in a creative family, he was influenced by his father, who was a teacher and an aficionado of silent films. Kurosawa was trained in painting before turning to film editing in his twenties, which laid the foundation for his cinematic career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beginning of Filmmaking Career</strong><br>\nKurosawa entered the film industry in 1936, working as an assistant director and later as a director in his own right. His directorial debut was \"Sanshiro Sugata\" in 1943. The early years of his career were marked by a prolific output of films, despite the constraints of World War II in Japan.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Post-War Success and Themes</strong><br>\nAfter World War II, Kurosawa's work reflected the changing times in Japan. His films often addressed issues of individualism, ethics, and social conflict. \"Drunken Angel\" (1948) and \"Stray Dog\" (1949) were notable films from this period that showcased his evolving style and thematic focus.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
