          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>International Acclaim</strong><br>\nKurosawa gained international fame with \"Rashomon\" (1950), which won the Golden Lion at the Venice Film Festival. This film introduced Western audiences to Japanese cinema and Kurosawa's narrative style, including his use of multiple perspectives to tell a story.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Film Technique</strong><br>\nKurosawa was known for his innovative techniques, such as using telephoto lenses for composition and experimenting with slow-motion effects. His method of shooting scenes from multiple angles helped create dynamic sequences that were influential in global cinema.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Collaboration with Toshiro Mifune</strong><br>\nToshiro Mifune was Kurosawa's favorite actor, appearing in 16 of his films. Their collaboration produced some of Kurosawa's best work, including \"Seven Samurai\" (1954) and \"Throne of Blood\" (1957), with Mifune's dynamic presence complementing Kurosawa's directorial vision.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
